#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Land this worktree's commits on origin/main.

    tools/land > /tmp/<lane>-land.log 2>&1; echo EXIT $? >> /tmp/<lane>-land.log

Join the common git directory's FIFO ticket queue, then fetch and rebase
onto origin/main (the files many lanes add to merge with the
`hirsel` merge driver, tools/buck2/merging.py), regenerate the build files
(`sync.py --if-stale`), build the workspace, run Clippy and check formatting
through Buck2, then push. The queue head lands a batch: its own commits, then
those of up to 15 waiting tickets in ticket order, checked once together and
pushed in one push; a conflicting ticket leaves the batch, and a red batch
bisects until its culprits are found (docs/landing.md). A waiting ticket
landed in a batch exits with its own landed commits. A push rejected because
main moved goes back to the rebase with full jitter and no attempt limit.
Every proposed tree is checked; a proven unchanged graph can narrow a retry to
incoming crates and their reverse dependencies. A red check refuses the push
and prints its first error.

Land runs no tests: the lane runs the tests its change can break, and
tools/main-runs runs the full suite on main every hour. Each push is appended
to `hirsel-landings.jsonl` in the common git directory, from which main-runs
names the lane of a commit.
"""

